.TH BT-INTERCOM 1
.SH NAME
bt-intercom \- half-duplex or full-duplex Bluetooth headset intercom
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bt-intercom
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bt-intercom
routes configured Bluetooth Classic headsets using BlueZ, PipeWire, and
WirePlumber. A microphone is routed to all other headsets, never back to
itself. Run it
as the same user that owns the PipeWire session; do not run it with
.BR sudo .
The default half-duplex SCO/A2DP transport changes only configured headset
profiles; the HFP transport leaves them unchanged. PipeWire links not owned by
the program are never modified.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Scan for nearby Bluetooth devices.
.TP
.BI pair " ADDRESS"
Pair, trust, and connect a headset. Pairing requires an interactive terminal.
.TP
.BI remove " ADDRESS"
Remove a headset from the saved intercom network. Bluetooth pairing is unchanged;
restart a running intercom or service for the change to take effect. Removing
the last address clears the saved network.
.TP
.BI status " ADDRESS" ...
Show each headset's Bluetooth name, duplex audio availability, and port counts.
.TP
.BI diagnose " [--passive] [ADDRESS ...]"
Test adapters and headsets on real hardware. Connects each headset, switches it
to A2DP and HFP, and measures how many simultaneous SCO links each controller
sustains, then summarizes BlueZ and PipeWire state (pairing, trust, controller
roles, codecs, capacity bounds). Uses the saved network when no address is
given. Stop any running intercom first: audio is interrupted, profile changes
are unsaved, and every headset ends with all profiles released (PipeWire off,
still connected) so none holds the controller's single A2DP stream.
.B \-\-passive
only inspects state without connecting or switching profiles. Also accepts
.B \-\-sco\-limit
and
.BR \-\-a2dp\-limit .
.TP
.BI run " [ADDRESS ...]"
Run the intercom. Explicit addresses are saved for future runs; if omitted, the
previously saved network is restored.
.TP
.BI completions " SHELL"
Write shell completion definitions to standard output. Supported shells include
Bash, Zsh, Fish, and Elvish.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI scan " --seconds SECONDS"
Set the scan duration from 1 through 300 seconds (default: 15).
.TP
.BI run " --interval SECONDS"
Set the routing update interval (default: 2 seconds; minimum: 1 second).
.TP
.BI run " --sco-limit N"
Set the maximum simultaneous SCO/eSCO links per Bluetooth controller (default:
1).
The HFP transport leaves profiles unchanged and warns if observed
headset usage exceeds the budget. Increase N only after verifying the adapter,
firmware, and headset capacity.
.TP
.BI run " --a2dp-limit N"
Set the assumed simultaneous A2DP streams per Bluetooth controller (default: 1).
In SCO/A2DP transport, listeners stream A2DP up to this limit; further listeners
on the same controller keep HFP/SCO and receive only. A controller therefore
supports --a2dp-limit plus --sco-limit headsets. Startup is rejected when the
network exceeds this. Increase N only after verifying the hardware.
.TP
.B run --no-connect
Do not connect headsets at startup or retry disconnected headsets every
30 seconds (both are the default).
Automatic connection uses BlueZ's default controller; multi-controller deployments need an
adapter-aware Bluetooth manager or manual select/connect in the same
bluetoothctl session.
.TP
.BI run " --mode MODE"
Select half-duplex (default) or full-duplex.
Half-duplex uses a play/pause input mapping for every headset and grants one
microphone at a time.
.TP
.BI run " --transport TRANSPORT"
Select sco-a2dp (default in half-duplex) or hfp (default in full-duplex). HFP
leaves profiles unchanged, so half-duplex mode with HFP does not reduce
listeners' SCO/eSCO usage.
SCO/A2DP requires half-duplex mode, PTT for every headset, and both
HFP/HSP and A2DP playback profiles. Listeners switch to A2DP before a talker
switches to HFP; profile indices and controller identities are discovered from
PipeWire.
Profile transitions are asynchronous and may interrupt audio. Profiles remain
in their last selected state on exit; owned links are released.
.PP
For full-duplex, provide at least two SCO/eSCO slots across the detected
controllers. By default this means one headset per independent controller;
--sco-limit can raise the per-controller budget after hardware verification.
Pair headsets to their intended controllers using bluetoothctl select before
pairing. Extra adapters do not redistribute existing bonds. Auracast requires
separate LE Audio broadcast hardware and software support and is not implemented.
.TP
.B run --host
Let the routing computer join the intercom with a wired (non-Bluetooth)
headset. Hold the spacebar (evdev KEY_SPACE) for true push-to-talk; the host
shares the floor and queue with the headsets.
.TP
.BI run " --host --host-keyboard " auto|/dev/input/eventX
Keyboard evdev device(s) for host PTT (default auto). Requires --host.
.TP
.BI run " --host --host-source " NODE " --host-sink " NODE
PipeWire node.name of the host microphone and speakers instead of the defaults.
.TP
.BI run " --ptt ADDRESS=/dev/input/eventX"
Override the default automatic matching of play/pause input devices by mapping
a headset to its Linux evdev push-to-talk input. Repeat once for every configured
headset. Only the headset's play/pause key-down events are used.
A headset that is not yet connected is warned about and attached once its input
device appears; startup fails if its input device matches ambiguously. Use ADDRESS=auto, or a lone auto, to request
automatic matching explicitly. Only KEY_PLAYPAUSE, KEY_PLAYCD, and KEY_PAUSECD
are recognised.
.PP
Headsets send each play/pause press as a tap, not a held key. Tap play
(KEY_PLAYCD) to open the microphone and pause (KEY_PAUSECD) to mute it; a
headset with one toggling play/pause key (KEY_PLAYPAUSE) alternates on each tap.
In half-duplex, requests
wait in first-in, first-out order while another headset transmits. Wait for a
double beep to confirm an active microphone route; pause cancels a
queued request or ends the current turn. All headsets share one queue
with one active talker.
Half-duplex routing failures stop the run and release owned links. External
links remain untouched; exclusivity applies only to this process's routes.
.PP
In full-duplex, every headset that has opened its microphone transmits.
Losing duplex audio resets a mapped headset's request; tap play again after
reconnecting.
.TP
.B run --dashboard
Show the live terminal dashboard on standard error. Requires an interactive
terminal.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show command help.
.TP
.B --verbose
Enable debug diagnostics for subprocess execution and routing decisions.
May be placed before or after a command. An explicit RUST_LOG setting
overrides the default log level selected by this option.
.TP
.B -v, --version
Show the program version and the UTC build datetime.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The saved headset network is stored at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bt-intercom/headsets ,
or
.IR ~/.config/bt-intercom/headsets
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or not absolute. Temporary PTT confirmation audio uses
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
when it is absolute and otherwise uses the system temporary directory.
.PP
Runtime log levels are controlled by
.BR RUST_LOG .
The default level is
.BR info ;
for example, set
.B RUST_LOG=warn
to show warnings and errors only. Logs are written to standard error and are
captured by the systemd journal when run as a user service.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The command exits with status 0 on success, 1 for runtime or semantic
configuration failures, and 2 for command-line syntax errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bluetoothctl (1),
.BR pw-cli (1),
.BR pw-dump (1),
.BR pw-play (1)
